brooklyn-based photographer andre d  wagner has been documenting the lives of  everyday americans in his neighborhood  and city for nearly a decade now  so we asked wagner to reflect on this  current moment in america  in this original essay on being a black  photographer  after the police murdered george floyd  on camera i found myself struggling with  the weight of the moment  and what to do as a black photographer  only a month prior  i was walking in my brooklyn  neighborhood and the policemen took my  camera for a gun  something that i'm sure my black skin  played a role in  [Music]  so on the second day of protest i  decided to go to lower manhattan to  photograph and be part of the  demonstrations  as i made my journey from brooklyn to  manhattan i routinely stopped at the  elevated subway and looked down  what used to be a community garden is  now an empty lot sold to developers a  few years ago  i took a few photographs noticing two  construction workers  as i advanced to filming my camera i  glanced down  and one of the construction workers was  picking up an iron ride and points it  directly at me  like a gun and then proceeds with a  shooting motion  also directed at me laughing i paused  for what seemed like an eternity  and then continued up the stairs and  onto the subway  as i sat on the subway and traveled over  to williamsburg bridge  i found myself thinking the same thing  that other black boys and men like me  think in these situations  people will never believe us unless i  get it on camera  when i moved to new york from omaha in  2011 to pursue a masters in social work  i also discovered photography and the  work of another midwestern photographer  gordon parks i instantly felt a personal  kinship with what he wrote about his  camera  to him it was a weapon against racism  and poverty i've had a camera in my hand  ever since  [Music]  as a black man i see the world through  black eyes and it's this blackness that  shapes my perspective  because of the real world immediacy of  my medium being out in the world  photographing real people  my black body helps me see truthfully  i've learned to operate in my skin  doing my craft the way i do it in order  to survive  when i set out on these streets to make  photographs they can stand on their own  i know it's a tall order i don't have  the luxury to only deal with the medium  i know for a fact that my camera can be  mistaken for a gun i know that some  people will think that i'm doing  something suspicious  and i know that my wife worries about me  coming home safely  but i also know the pure joy of kids  playing in the streets  or the special bond share between the  father and daughter and i definitely  know the feeling of the young brother  lonely in the sea of faces on 34th  street  [Music]  after doing some more research i found  more information about the old community  garden  a black woman by the name of miss  hansley moved from north carolina  to new york in the 60s in search of a  better life  she landed in bushwick brooklyn and  founded what would be called the secret  garden  the same plot of land that these two men  stood on as they made a joke out of me  i used to think that being black would  be a burden as a photographer  i quickly changed my stance because now  my life in this gift of sight will be my  legacy  i became a black photographer in spite  of  i've had real moments of affirmation  looking through this viewfinder  nothing brings me more joy than to  organize the real world around me into  distinct moments  trying to get at the heart of anyone  willing to pause and look at my  photographs  it seems to me that one of the beautiful  aspects about art is that it places us  face to face with our history  in america we want to sweep our history  underneath the rug  but any real work of art won't let you
 

